(2847840, 388625, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Pretty picture...<br/> <br/>I like the sky.  For a featureless sky you did well, adding a little interst to it with the polarizer.  I''d also vote to eliminate the white spot in the water.  It''s too small to see what it is, and winds up being distracting.  <br/> <br/>What I see here, is two sets of leading lines.  The lamp posts take us in one direction, to the right.  The shoreline tries to guide us to the left, and a tug of war results.  As the viewer, I wind up trying to decide what the real subject is.  The buildings are a little soft.  A smaller f-stop would help here.  The lamps are sharp but lead us almost off teh edge of teh frame, and then we have to cross over them to follow the shoreline and road.<br/> <br/>My focus keeps winding up at the construction site near the white and red buildings, which is close to center of the picture.<br/> <br/>If you could have gotten a bit to the left of this vantage point, the lamps would have lead the viewer in concert with the shore line and that might help.<br/> <br/>Overall this is a pretty picture that I enjoyed looking at.  Wish I had one like this in my summer vacation book this year.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Pretty picture... I like the sky.  For a featureless sky you did well, adding a little interst to it with the polarizer.  I''d also vote to eliminate the white spot in the water.  It''s too small to see what it is, and winds up being distracting.   What I see here, is two sets of leading lines.  The lamp posts take us in one direction, to the right.  The shoreline tries to guide us to the left, and a tug of war results.  As the viewer, I wind up trying to decide what the real subject is.  The buildings are a little soft.  A smaller f-stop would help here.  The lamps are sharp but lead us almost off teh edge of teh frame, and then we have to cross over them to follow the shoreline and road. My focus keeps winding up at the construction site near the white and red buildings, which is close to center of the picture. If you could have gotten a bit to the left of this vantage point, the lamps would have lead the viewer in concert with the shore line and that might help. Overall this is a pretty picture that I enjoyed looking at.  Wish I had one like this in my summer vacation book this year.', '2006-09-19 16:00:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2847916, 391611, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Focus is nice and sharp.  Depth of field is used well to remove background distractions.<br/> <br/>Color, saturation, hue, brightness and contrast are done well.<br/> <br/>I would like to see either more, or less, frog body here.  The eye starts at the frog''s eye, but is soon lead by the ridge on the frog''s body up and out of the picture.  Result is that it is not a natural end point.  Postioning the frog so that either the entire body is included; or that only the head is present would have worked better for me.<br/> <br/>Congratulations on making the top half of the class.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Focus is nice and sharp.  Depth of field is used well to remove background distractions. Color, saturation, hue, brightness and contrast are done well. I would like to see either more, or less, frog body here.  The eye starts at the frog''s eye, but is soon lead by the ridge on the frog''s body up and out of the picture.  Result is that it is not a natural end point.  Postioning the frog so that either the entire body is included; or that only the head is present would have worked better for me. Congratulations on making the top half of the class.', '2006-09-19 16:40:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2848021, 390925, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Color, saturation and hue are good.  Brightness and contrast are OK.<br/> <br/>Lighting makes the picture look a little flat.  I can tell by what I see that there must be a lot of texture there, I just don''t see it with this lighting.  <br/> <br/>You''ve followed the rule of thirds, but what happens here (at least for me) is the eye follows the windows to the the bright sky above the bit too soft trees.  Once there, the sky is pale and featureless.  <br/> <br/>The windows are a nice effect, they just keep leading us to nowhere.  I believe that is why you received the "forced" comment below.  The windows command so much attention, that they really need a powerful subject when you get there.<br/> <br/>I have no idea which way this builing faces, but a sunset in that window with some fill flash on this side, would be killer for me.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Color, saturation and hue are good.  Brightness and contrast are OK. Lighting makes the picture look a little flat.  I can tell by what I see that there must be a lot of texture there, I just don''t see it with this lighting.   You''ve followed the rule of thirds, but what happens here (at least for me) is the eye follows the windows to the the bright sky above the bit too soft trees.  Once there, the sky is pale and featureless.   The windows are a nice effect, they just keep leading us to nowhere.  I believe that is why you received the "forced" comment below.  The windows command so much attention, that they really need a powerful subject when you get there. I have no idea which way this builing faces, but a sunset in that window with some fill flash on this side, would be killer for me.', '2006-09-19 17:38:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(2848927, 389219, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Interesting picture....<br/> <br/>The horizon is just about along the upper rule of thirds line.<br/> <br/>The horizon is a little curved.  Wide angle lens?  I see a cloudy sky, so no shadows makes sense.  However, the sky near horizion is so bright as to be blown out and featureless. The spray winds up dissapearing into it.  I would have liked it a bit better, if that hadn''t happened.<br/> <br/>Otherwise color, saturation, and hue, as well as focus and depth of field work very well.<br/> <br/>I like this picture.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Interesting picture.... The horizon is just about along the upper rule of thirds line. The horizon is a little curved.  Wide angle lens?  I see a cloudy sky, so no shadows makes sense.  However, the sky near horizion is so bright as to be blown out and featureless. The spray winds up dissapearing into it.  I would have liked it a bit better, if that hadn''t happened. Otherwise color, saturation, and hue, as well as focus and depth of field work very well. I like this picture.', '2006-09-19 22:39:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(2849138, 390691, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>I''m not quite sure which bird is supposed to be the "odd" one.  I''m torn between the one on the left, and the one on the right.<br/> <br/>What I do find is my eye usually winds up somewhere between the bird on the left and the two in the center.<br/> <br/>For me, this picture would have worked better if the extra (I assume right hand bird) were cropped out.  Also, the sky here is a featureless canvas.  It needs something.  Darker, clouds, birds flying, something.  As it is, this is a lot of space to have no texture, and nothing going on.<br/> <br/>Lighting, color, saturation, and hue are done well.  The picture looks a bit oversharpened as seen in the halo along the front of the neck of the left-most bird.</td>', 'Critique Club Review: I''m not quite sure which bird is supposed to be the "odd" one.  I''m torn between the one on the left, and the one on the right. What I do find is my eye usually winds up somewhere between the bird on the left and the two in the center. For me, this picture would have worked better if the extra (I assume right hand bird) were cropped out.  Also, the sky here is a featureless canvas.  It needs something.  Darker, clouds, birds flying, something.  As it is, this is a lot of space to have no texture, and nothing going on. Lighting, color, saturation, and hue are done well.  The picture looks a bit oversharpened as seen in the halo along the front of the neck of the left-most bird.', '2006-09-19 23:51:35.000000', NULL, 0),
